  "slug": "activation-sars-cov-46ec",
  "summary": "Protein language models and AlphaFold3 structures are now paired with activation and interaction data to predict receptor-linked molecular outcomes.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is learned prediction of receptor-linked molecular behavior from structural and sequence-derived representations. A preprint couples AlphaFold3 receptor structures and protein language model embeddings with pS6-IP-Seq activation data to predict odorant activation. A separate preprint analyzes ESM-2 attention maps of the adenosine A2A receptor, reporting directed asymmetry that routes information from the extracellular ligand site toward the allosteric hierarchy. eSIG-Net, a published sequence-based language model with contrastive learning, predicts how single mutations rewire protein interactions and is reported to outperform comparison methods. Dango, a published self-attention hypergraph neural network, addresses higher-order trigenic interactions in yeast. What is new is attention-level analysis of receptor allostery and extension of these predictions to mutation-level interaction effects, while the dominant thread remains receptor-directed prediction rather than a uniform shared methodology.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-04-30"
}
